Abstract = {The aim of this article is to analyse the consequences of the German
   government's 1998 decision to stop the production of nuclear energy on
   the long-standing relations between economic interest groups and
   governmental actors. The authors argue that the political paradigm
   change led to a significant change in the structures of the relationship
   between these actors. The different periods of the overall German policy
   change are examined, analysing the influence of actors' ideas and
   strategies. While the successive policy changes in the 1980s and 1990s
   triggered only minor adaptations of interest group strategies, the 1998
   decision abruptly broke the strong links between interest groups and
   governmental actors. As a result, interest groups reach out for the
   establishment of new international and European networks. The article
   shows, however, that these new strategies are still very much influenced
   by the national structures in which they originated.},
